(   [Problem Statement]: Write an ANSI C program to implement Selection Sort, explaining why it performs exactly N\(N-1\)/2 comparisons and its instability.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Selection Sort: Mechanics, Complexity, and Instability:) Tj
T*
(   1. Algorithmic Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - Divides array into two subarrays: sorted prefix \(left\) and unsorted suffix \(right\).) Tj
T*
(   - In each pass 'i', find the minimum element in unsorted suffix arr[i...N-1].) Tj
T*
(   - Swap this minimum element with arr[i], expanding the sorted prefix by one element.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Fixed Number of Comparisons:) Tj
T*
(   - Pass 1: \(N - 1\) comparisons.) Tj
T*
(   - Pass 2: \(N - 2\) comparisons.) Tj
T*
(   - Pass N-1: 1 comparison.) Tj
T*
(   - Total Comparisons: \(N - 1\) + \(N - 2\) + ... + 1 = N * \(N - 1\) / 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Regardless of whether the input is sorted, reverse-sorted, or random, Selection Sort ALWAYS) Tj
T*
(   performs exactly N\(N-1\)/2 comparisons \(O\(N^2\) in all cases\).) Tj
T*
(   - Advantage: Makes at most \(N - 1\) memory swaps \(minimal write operations, valuable for flash) Tj
T*
(   memory\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Cause of Instability:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider sorting pairs [4a, 4b, 2]:) Tj
T*
(   Minimum is 2 at index 2. Swapping 2 with arr[0] \(4a\) yields: [2, 4b, 4a].) Tj
T*
(   Notice 4b now precedes 4a! The relative order of equal elements was corrupted, making Selection) Tj
T*
(   Sort UNSTABLE!) Tj
T*
ET

