(   [Problem Statement]: Write an ANSI C program to implement Insertion Sort, explaining its adaptive nature on nearly sorted arrays and stability.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Insertion Sort: Adaptive Sorting and Stability:) Tj
T*
(   1. Algorithmic Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - Operates identically to sorting playing cards in hand.) Tj
T*
(   - Array is divided into sorted partition arr[0...i-1] and unsorted partition arr[i...N-1].) Tj
T*
(   - Element 'key = arr[i]' is extracted and compared backward with elements in the sorted) Tj
T*
(   partition.) Tj
T*
(   - Elements greater than 'key' are shifted one position to the right.) Tj
T*
(   - 'key' is inserted into its proper vacated position.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Adaptive Nature and Stability:) Tj
T*
(   - Adaptive: If the array is already sorted, the inner while loop terminates on the very first) Tj
T*
(   comparison \(arr[j] > key is false\).) Tj
T*
(   Best-case time complexity is O\(N\) linear time!) Tj
T*
(   Highly efficient for nearly-sorted datasets or appending small elements to sorted arrays.) Tj
T*
(   - Strictly Stable: Since elements are shifted only when strictly greater \(arr[j] > key\), equal) Tj
T*
(   elements maintain their relative positions.) Tj
T*
