(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Variable-Length Arrays \(VLAs\) in C99, detailing stack allocation, sizeof evaluation, and security hazards.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Variable-Length Arrays \(VLAs\) in C99:) Tj
T*
(   1. Language Specification:) Tj
T*
(   - Introduced in ISO C99 to allow runtime dimensioning of local stack arrays:) Tj
T*
(   void process_data\(int n\) {) Tj
T*
(   double buffer[n]; /* Array size 'n' evaluated at runtime */) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
(   - Stack Allocation: VLAs are allocated dynamically on the call stack by adjusting the stack) Tj
T*
(   pointer \(RSP/ESP\) at runtime \(similar to alloca\(\)\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Dynamic sizeof Evaluation:) Tj
T*
(   - For standard arrays, sizeof is evaluated entirely at compile-time.) Tj
T*
(   - For VLAs, sizeof is evaluated AT RUNTIME:) Tj
T*
(   size_t bytes = sizeof\(buffer\); /* Computes n * sizeof\(double\) at runtime */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Security and Reliability Hazards:) Tj
T*
(   - Lack of Out-of-Memory Detection: Unlike malloc\(\), which returns NULL when memory is) Tj
T*
(   exhausted, allocating an oversized VLA \(e.g. n = 10,000,000\) causes an immediate, catastrophic) Tj
T*
(   STACK OVERFLOW and program crash!) Tj
T*
(   - Denial of Service \(DoS\): If 'n' is controlled by user input without bounds checking,) Tj
